Begin quantitative and technical literacy from zero (@ql-reading-from-zero: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @quantitative-literacy, #source @ql-source-trades-1, #source @ql-source-trades-2, #source @ql-source-trades-3, #source @ql-source-prealgebra, #source @ql-source-algebra, #quantitative-literacy "foundations/Quantity-and-reference.lingua#r_65GAJ89AZRG6VYVXDC43NBT0DK") { r_0RVVPMXQQDS96N2BCGHD50N2NN
Start with Math for Trades, Volume 1, read completely with the exercises and final practice test. Work its numerical examples on paper, then use a calculator to check. Prealgebra 2e chapters 1–11 provide a fuller arithmetic-to-algebra book; read the whole book if those foundations are new, or use the named chapters to repair gaps discovered in the review.

Continue through the linked Records and complete Math for Trades, Volumes 2 and 3. Alongside them, study the selected Algebra and Trigonometry 2e chapters. The same idea can appear in several books: read a second explanation when the first is unclear, and demonstrate it on a new problem before moving on. No previous course or programming language is assumed.

| Stage | Substantial reading | Understanding and work |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Numbers and comparisons | Math for Trades 1 complete; Prealgebra 1–7 and 10 as needed | signs, fractions, decimals, denominators, percentages, and numerical scale |
| Units and equations | Math for Trades 2 complete; SI Brochure 2–5; Prealgebra 8 | physical quantities, conversions, equations, and explicit constraints |
| Functions and spatial reasoning | Math for Trades 3 complete; Algebra and Trigonometry 1–4, 6–7, 8.1–8.3, 10.1–10.2 and 10.5–10.8, linear systems in 11 | tables, graphs, logarithms, geometry, trigonometry, complex-number notation, vectors, and independent equations |
| Drawings and accounts | Basic Blueprint Reading complete; The Art of Insight Parts I–II; Broman and Woo complete | technical views, specification versus measurement, flows, yields, and recoverable data |
| Measurement and model criticism | JCGM GUM-1 complete; VIM definitions in context; The Art of Insight Part III | uncertainty, estimation, scaling, approximations, and limits of inference |
| Advanced command | both Mahajan books complete; NIST TN 1297 complete, including appendices | independent derivation, source comparison, reproducible studies, and criticism and revision |

Section numbers refer to the editions described in the source Records. Use the named topic when a web revision changes numbering. Complete means read the explanations and work the problems, not open each page once. Restricted instructor answer materials are not required; the published questions, available answers, independent calculations, and these original studies support self-study.

The later mathematical exercises are deliberately demanding. Return to the earlier algebra, functions, and geometric work where they reveal a gap. Full analysis, probability, numerical analysis, physical mechanisms, and metrology deepen through their own subject material; this path gives the common quantitative language and a substantial advanced practice with it.

**Continue:** [A number needs a quantity and a reference](foundations/Quantity-and-reference.lingua#r_65GAJ89AZRG6VYVXDC43NBT0DK).

